[
  {
    "sha": "3c0c68b3d0397ee2e8344b77e9ed715132fef6ca",
    "node_id": "C_kwDOTeTpgNoAKDNjMGM2OGIzZDAzOTdlZTJlODM0NGI3N2U5ZWQ3MTUxMzJmZWY2Y2E",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "charles hoskinson",
        "email": "charles.hoskinson@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-07-26T17:50:21Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "charles hoskinson",
        "email": "charles.hoskinson@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-07-26T17:50:21Z"
      },
      "message": "docs(research): experimental results — two findings reorder the roadmap\n\nRan the read-only Stage 0 experiments against the live 4.3.3 indexer.\nThree open questions settled by measurement instead of argument.\n\nR1 answers the E0.4 gate, and the answer is the opposite of the assumption\nthe research was ranked on. We are CPU-bound, not I/O-bound: 70% of one\ncore, but only 27 KB/s of PHYSICAL disk reads against 3,953 read syscalls\nper second, with 23 GB of page cache resident over an 88 GB node store. The\npage cache already serves essentially every read. Ira's 5.2-23.6x came from\na profile that was 67.9% I/O; prefetch exists to get data off disk before\nit is needed and our data is already in memory. So hint-driven prefetch is\ndemoted, and what gets promoted is pipelining plus anything that reduces\nthe NUMBER of lookups, because each lookup is now syscall and B-tree CPU\nrather than disk latency.\n\nR2 retracts my own earlier conclusion. I raised cache_size to 16 MiB\nobjects, measured no sustained gain, reverted, and called the lever spent.\nThat does not survive R1: with 64.5% of CPU inside the quadratic clone, a\ngain confined to the remaining third would have been invisible, and the\nin-process cache is precisely what avoids those 4,000 syscalls/sec. The\nearlier negative is recorded as unreliable rather than as evidence, and\ncache_size must be re-tested after the Rc backport.\n\nR3 resolves the GC disagreement by checking our tag rather than HEAD:\nv4.3.3 has features = [\"layout-v2\"], HEAD has [\"layout-v2\", \"gc-v1\"]. Our\nindexer has NO ledger-DB garbage collection, corroborated by zero GC log\nlines in 30 minutes. One lane was right for our version; the other had read\nHEAD. This also explains the disk trajectory: ~1 GB/hour with nothing ever\nreclaimed.\n\nR4 confirms Stage 4's premise and sharpens it. At v4.3.3 application.rs,\nline 394 computes ledger_state.root() only to compare it against the node's\nvalue and bail on mismatch, line 404 does the same for the zswap root,\nledger_state_root appears zero times in the migrations so no such column\nexists, and the persisted zswap column is populated from the block rather\nthan from our computation. Both computed roots are pure validation, so\ndeferring them changes no stored byte. The Stage 4 decision now rests on\nverified code rather than a reported claim.",
