      "message": "docs(research): roadmap v2 — the research changed the goal\n\nResearched how Erigon, Reth, Besu, Aptos, Solana, Sui and Substreams solve\nthis. The conclusion is that parallel execution is the wrong target, and\nthe evidence is specific rather than aesthetic.\n\nThe governing find is Ira (Visa Research, arXiv:2601.21286), which states\nour problem verbatim: a replayer already knows future access patterns,\nwhich is exactly what optimal replay needs. Measured on 100,800 mainnet\nblocks against unmodified reth: 5.2x with ONE prefetch thread, 23.6x with\n16, executor sequential throughout. All parallelism was I/O prefetch, and\nthe mechanism is sorting rather than threading. It is bit-identical by\nconstruction, not by proof: hints are advisory and a crash-on-miss cache\nhalts instead of diverging.\n\nBlock-STM is rejected on our own numbers. Its guarantee is sound and it\ndelivers 16-18x at 32 threads, but every block size the paper evaluates is\n1,000 to 50,000 transactions and we run 0.66 per block. The same paper\nmeasures up to 30% overhead on inherently sequential workloads, so we would\npay the 30% and collect none of the 16x. Sealevel needs a transaction\nformat change, which for replayed history means the history is no longer\nthe history. Sui's fast path optimises reaching agreement on an ordering we\nalready have. Substreams' segmented fold requires associativity our apply()\ndoes not have. Each rejection is recorded with the reason so nobody\nre-proposes it.\n\nTwo structural wins replace them, both bit-identical. Sorted write staging,\nwhich matters unusually here because a content-addressed store makes every\nkey a random hash and random B-tree inserts are the exact pathology\nErigon's ETL exists to defeat. And pipelining deserialization, which is a\npure function of block bytes and entirely off the dependency chain - the\ndirect heir to the 64.5% profile finding.\n\nStage 4 is escalated rather than decided: two of four per-block roots are\ncomputed, compared and discarded, never persisted. Deferring them to batch\nboundaries changes no output byte, only the validation model, and Erigon\nand Reth both do exactly this during historical sync. Plausibly the largest\nsingle win, but it reduces per-block checking on an artifact meant as\nrelease evidence, so it is the owner's call.\n\nRecords an unresolved conflict rather than papering over it: two lanes\ndisagree on whether 4.3.3 has ledger-DB GC, having read different\nrevisions. Must be settled by inspection before anyone tunes gc_bound.\n\nAlso carries an incidental upstream correctness finding: get_root_count\nselects count(1), returning row existence rather than the stored refcount\ncolumn, against a storage-core test asserting the latter.",
