      "message": "docs(research): staged roadmap for chain-indexer parallelism\n\nWritten against a live perf profile of our own running indexer, not from\nreading the code. The measurement changed the plan.\n\n64.5% of CPU is hashbrown::RawTable clone+drop. Proof verification, the\nobvious parallelism target on a ZK chain, is 0.28%. The cause is an O(N^2)\ndeep clone of key_to_child_repr in storage-core arena.rs:1160-1166: the\ndeserializer recurses once per node and copies an N-entry HashMap at every\nlevel, once per transaction. Bimodal block times corroborate it, 0.185s for\nempty blocks against 1.87s for blocks carrying transactions.\n\nSo the first win is not parallelism. Upstream already fixed this on the\nledger-9 line by making the field an Rc; the fix is absent from ledger-8.1.0,\nstorage-core 1.2.1-rc.1 and crates.io 1.2.0, which means upgrading the\npublished image does not get it and a one-line backport does. Roughly 1.9x.\n\nStage 0 is experiments only, gating everything else, and it starts by\nremoving the quadratic term because that changes the profile every later\nparallelism decision depends on. Stage 1 is Quint modelling: the obligation\nis that any parallel pipeline produces a bit-identical result to the\nsequential fold, so the admissible reorderings get proven before concurrent\nRust is written, each with a falsifiability control that must fire.\n\nStage 4 (intra-block parallelism) is included and pre-emptively rejected:\nat 0.66 transactions per block there is nothing to parallelise within a\nblock. Recording why is more useful than leaving it as an open idea.\n\nThe roadmap declines to promise 20x on 20 cores. The ledger fold is a\nsequential dependency no scheduler removes; realistic is ~1.9x from the\nbackport and maybe 2.5-3x cumulative.\n\nAlso records the more urgent problem: 4.3.3 has no ledger-DB GC, every\nblock's state is a permanent root, and the databases are 161 GB at\nhalf-height and should be expected to double. Throughput is irrelevant if\nthe disk fills first.",
